spec secret_share_reconstruct

description:
  Additive secret sharing over a prime field `GF(p)`. `share` splits a secret
  `x` into `n >= 2` shares by drawing the first `n-1` uniformly at random and
  fixing the last as `x - sum(others)`. `reconstruct` is the field sum of all
  `n` shares. The privacy contract: any `n-1` of the shares are jointly
  uniform and independent of `x`; only the full set determines `x`.

  Covers `tpt-crypto-mpc::field_share` (`share_secret`, `share_secret_2`,
  `reconstruct`, and the local `Share::add` / `sub` / `scale` / `neg`
  homomorphisms).

inputs:
  x : FieldElement<P>   // secret
  n : usize             // party count, >= 2 (public)

outputs:
  shares : Vec<Share>   // length n
  // and for the round-trip:
  x_rt   : FieldElement<P>   // reconstruct(shares)

assumes:
  - the RNG is a CSPRNG
  - n >= 2 (otherwise `Error::InvalidLength`)

ensures:
  // correctness: reconstruction is exact
  reconstruct(share(x, n)) == x
  // additive homomorphism under local ops (no interaction)
  reconstruct(zip_with(add, share(x,n), share(y,n))) == x + y
  reconstruct(map(scale(c), share(x,n)))             == c * x   // c public
  // privacy: any proper subset of shares is independent of x
  for all S subset of [0,n) with |S| < n :
      distribution(shares[S] | x)  ==  uniform over F^{|S|}
  // constant-time: reconstruction and local ops do not branch on share values
  execution_trace ⟂ (x, shares)

non_leaks:
  - `reconstruct` is a fixed-length field-addition fold; iteration count is `n`,
    independent of the share values
  - `Share::add` / `sub` / `scale` / `neg` are branch-free field ops
  - the sharing loop draws exactly `n-1` uniform elements then one subtraction;
    trip count is `n`, not data-dependent (rejection sampling inside
    `F::random` retries only on a non-canonical raw draw, which is independent
    of `x`)

verification:
  // tests/kat.rs `share_reconstruct_kat` and tests/props.rs:
  // reconstruct(share(x)) == x, plus Beaver-multiplied shares reconstruct to
  // `a*b`, over random inputs.
